`Object.freeze` is shallow. It locks down the properties of the object itself—you can't reassign `cfg.db` or add keys to `cfg`. But it doesn't touch what those properties point to. `cfg.db` is still a live object, so `cfg.db.host = 'y'` mutates that nested object freely.

To freeze the whole tree, you need to recursively freeze:

```javascript
function deepFreeze(obj) {
  Object.freeze(obj);
  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj).forEach(prop => {
    if (obj[prop] !== null && (typeof obj[prop] === 'object' || typeof obj[prop] === 'function') && !Object.isFrozen(obj[prop])) {
      deepFreeze(obj[prop]);
    }
  });
  return obj;
}
```

Cost of missing this: config mutation that looks impossible but happens silently. Data corruption without a trace.
